    navJourney:"Coffee's journey",
    journeyTitle:"The journey of the coffee bean",
    journeySub:"A fruit that goats ate before people did. Once called the work of the devil, banned, hoarded, its seeds smuggled across oceans - until it became the most widely drunk beverage on Earth after water.",
    badgeLegend:"Legend", badgeDoc:"Documented", tlLink:"See on the map", sourcesTitle:"Sources", srcLabel:"Sources",
    timeline:[
      {year:"Around 850", kind:"legend", country:"ethiopia", title:"The goatherd and the goats that would not sleep",
       body:"In the highlands of Ethiopia, a goatherd named Kaldi noticed that his goats would not sleep one night. They leapt, bleated and buzzed with strange energy after eating the red berries of a hillside shrub. Curious, Kaldi tried them himself and felt wide awake. He carried the berries to a nearby monastery. The monk who heard the story called them the work of the devil and threw a handful into the fire - but from the embers rose a smell no one had ever breathed before. They raked out the charred beans, crushed them, poured on hot water, and that night the whole monastery prayed until dawn without tiring. This lovely story was only written down in the 16th-17th centuries, nearly 800 years after it supposedly happened, so historians file it under legend. Yet a truth stands behind it: Arabica still grows wild in Ethiopia's mountain forests, and Ethiopians keep one of the oldest coffee ceremonies in the world.", refs:[1,3,4]},
      {year:"12th-15th century", kind:"doc", title:"Yemen: where humans learned to drink coffee",
       body:"Before it was a drink, coffee was a medicine - and a secret of holy men. The earliest written record of coffee cultivation comes from 12th-century Yemen. Three centuries later, in Sufi monasteries high in the Yemeni mountains, monks began roasting the beans, grinding them, brewing them with hot water and drinking the result to stay awake through long nights of prayer. They called it 'qahwa'. From that word, almost every language on Earth took its name for coffee: coffee, cafe, kahve, ca phe. Ethiopia gave birth to the plant; Yemen taught the world how to drink it.", refs:[1,2]},
      {year:"1475", kind:"doc", title:"The first coffeehouse - and a nervous governor",
       body:"When coffee left the monastery for the street, it created something new: a place where strangers sat together for hours without alcohol. The first documented coffeehouse opened in Constantinople (today's Istanbul) in 1475. Within decades, coffeehouses filled Cairo, Damascus and Mecca. People came for news, chess, storytelling, and talk of both God and politics. In 1511 the governor of Mecca shut down every coffeehouse in the city because satirical verses about him were being written there - a ban the sultan in Cairo soon overturned. From the very start, coffee was the drink of free conversation.", refs:[3,9]},
      {year:"16th century", kind:"doc", title:"Guarding the treasure by roasting it",
       body:"Yemen knew it was holding a treasure. Under Ottoman control, no coffee bean could leave port unless it had been roasted or parboiled first - so no one could plant it. For more than a century, the entire world bought its coffee through a single gate: the port of Mocha on the Red Sea. The name travelled with the beans to Europe, attached itself to the chocolatey taste Europeans found in the cup, and to this day 'mocha' sits on the menu of every cafe.", refs:[2]},
      {year:"17th century", kind:"doc", country:"indonesia", title:"To Europe - and out of Yemen",
       body:"Coffee entered Europe through Venetian merchants, was first eyed with suspicion as a 'Muslim drink', then swept the continent. Coffeehouses opened in London, Paris and Vienna and became places where merchants signed deals, thinkers argued and the first newspapers were born. But the bigger turning point happened more quietly: the Dutch obtained living seedlings and carried them to the island of Java. For the first time in centuries, coffee grew outside Ethiopia and Yemen. The monopoly was broken. From Java, the coffee plant set off across the colonies of Asia, Africa and the Americas.", refs:[1]},
      {year:"1727", kind:"legend", country:"brazil", title:"An empire that began with a bouquet",
       body:"As the story goes, in 1727 a Portuguese officer named Francisco de Melo Palheta was sent to French Guiana to settle a border dispute - with a secret mission to bring back coffee. The French guarded their plants closely. Palheta left with a bouquet from the governor's wife, and hidden inside were coffee seeds. Embellished or not, coffee reached Brazil around that time. A century and a half later, Brazil became the largest coffee grower on the planet, and it has held that title without interruption ever since - more than 150 years, unchallenged.", refs:[1,8]},
      {year:"1857", kind:"doc", country:"vietnam", title:"Coffee reaches Vietnam in a missionary's luggage",
       body:"Coffee came to Vietnam not on a merchant ship but in the luggage of French missionaries. In 1857 the first Arabica plants were tried around churches in Ninh Binh, Thanh Hoa, Nghe An and Ha Tinh - at first almost a hobby for homesick foreigners. It took more than 30 years, until 1888, for the first plantations to be set up in Ninh Binh and Quang Binh. But the cold, damp north was not where the coffee plant wanted to stay.", refs:[5,6]},
      {year:"1908-1925", kind:"doc", country:"vietnam", title:"Robusta finds the Central Highlands",
       body:"Arabica in Vietnam gave little fruit and fell ill easily. In 1908 the French brought in two more species: Robusta and Excelsa. They tried them in many places, and by 1925 Robusta was planted in the Central Highlands. There, on red basalt soil, under the highland sun and monsoon rain, this hardy plant grew fast, resisted disease and bore more fruit than anywhere it had been tried. The land and the plant had found each other - though it would take another half century, and two wars, before the country understood what that meeting meant.", refs:[6,7]},
      {year:"1986", kind:"doc", country:"vietnam", title:"A starting point close to zero",
       body:"In 1986 all of Vietnam had about 50,000 hectares of coffee, yielding 18,400 tonnes - just over 300,000 bags, so little that on the world coffee map Vietnam barely existed. It was also the year the Doi Moi reforms began. Farmers were given land and the right to sell what they grew, and the Central Highlands had something no other place had: hundreds of thousands of hectares of red soil suited to Robusta, and plants that had been rooted there for 60 years. From this starting point, one of the fastest leaps in the history of world agriculture was about to begin.", refs:[6]},
      {year:"2024-2025", kind:"doc", country:"vietnam", title:"Second in the world - and still moving",
       body:"Less than two decades after Doi Moi, Vietnam had climbed to second in the world. In the 2024-2025 season it grew about 29 million bags - nearly 100 times the 1986 figure - behind only Brazil, and the largest Robusta grower on the planet. The bean that travelled from Ethiopia to Yemen, to Java, to Brazil, now has another great home in the Central Highlands. And the story is still being written: a new generation of Vietnamese farmers and roasters is proving that Robusta is not just plentiful, but can be very good.", refs:[6,8]},
    ],
